"""
Pulse extraction using the POS algorithm.

Reference: W. Wang, A. C. den Brinker, S. Stuijk and G. de Haan,
"Algorithmic Principles of Remote PPG," IEEE Trans. Biomed. Eng., 2017.

Changes vs. the original script (all aimed at reducing heart-rate MAE):

1. Fixed off-by-one sliding-window bug (window was l-1 samples, not l).
2. Added a Butterworth band-pass filter (default 0.7-3.5 Hz = 42-210 bpm)
   applied to the POS pulse signal before spectral analysis. This is the
   step every reference POS/CHROM implementation includes and the
   original script was missing -- it removes low-frequency illumination
   drift and high-frequency motion noise that otherwise dominate the PSD
   peak search.
3. Frames with no detected face are no longer silently dropped. The last
   known face box is reused for up to `--max_gap` consecutive misses so
   the sample index still corresponds to a fixed 1/framerate time step.
   Dropping frames without accounting for it silently changes the
   effective sampling rate used later to convert Hz -> bpm.
4. Welch PSD now uses a Hamming window (sharper spectral peak for a
   single dominant frequency) instead of flattop (designed for accurate
   *amplitude* estimation of multiple tones, not sharp peak-picking).
5. Added epsilon-guard against divide-by-zero when a channel's windowed
   mean is ~0.
6. Non-blocking, optional plotting (saved to disk instead of plt.show()),
   so the script can run headlessly/in batch for evaluation.
7. Refactored into functions; added --ground_truth_hr so you can print
   the MAE (and see it accumulate across a batch of videos) instead of
   just eyeballing the number.
8. CLI flags for start/end frame, frequency band, window length factor,
   and output paths, instead of hard-coded constants.
9. Face detection/ROI replaced: dlib's frontal HOG detector + crude
   bounding box -> MediaPipe Face Landmarker (478 landmarks) + a
   forehead+cheeks skin mask built from the face-oval hull minus the
   eyes/eyebrows/lips hulls. This does not touch the POS math, the
   band-pass filter, or the HR estimation (all unchanged) -- it only
   changes which pixels feed into mean_rgb, and how much non-skin
   background/hair/eye/mouth signal gets averaged in. This also fixes
   the MISSING-frame dropout problem from dlib's frontal-only detector,
   since MediaPipe tolerates head-turns, blur, and uneven lighting far
   better.
10. Added an experimental "Modified POS" combination step (SVD-based
    projection instead of alpha-tuning), selectable via --pos_combine
    {alpha, svd}. Default remains "alpha" (the original, validated
    method) -- "svd" underperformed it on synthetic tests here and is
    offered as an opt-in to A/B test on real footage, not a proven
    upgrade. See pos_algorithm()'s docstring for the tested numbers.

Requires: pip install mediapipe (the face_landmarker.task model bundle
is downloaded automatically on first run to --face_model's path).

Notes on validating MAE:
The two .mat files that came with this project (pulse_test_frame-0-15.mat,
pulse_rPPG_video_frame-0-15.mat) are *saved outputs* of a previous run
(pulse signal + estimated bpm), not ground-truth references, so they
can't be used to compute a real MAE. To actually tune/verify MAE you
need paired (video, true HR) data -- e.g. a public dataset like
UBFC-rPPG or PURE (which ship synchronized fingertip-PPG/ECG HR), or
your own pulse-oximeter log recorded alongside the video.
"""

